    <statement><p>Compute the length of the curve <m>{\bf r\it(t)} = \left\lt  -3 t,9 t+ 1,-2 t + 3\right&gt;</m> over the interval <m>0 \le t \le 6</m></p>
<p><m>L=</m> <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>58.1722</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x(t)=-3 t,y(t)=9 t + 1, z(t)= -2 t + 3</m> hence
<me>x'(t) = -3,\qquad y'(t)=9,\qquad z'(t)=-2</me>
We use the Arc Length Formula to obtain:



<me>L=\int_0^{6}\|(\bf r\it'(t)\|\,dt=
   \int_0^{6}\sqrt{x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2+z'(t)^2}\,dt</me>



<me>=\int_0^{6}\sqrt{(-3)^2+9^2+(-2)^2}\,dt=
   6 \sqrt{94}</me>



</solution>
